       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">DEFENCE</span> · Defence (aggregate) · 2026-09-25 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India's Drone Sector Sees Explosive Revenue Growth Under Make in India</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Drones</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Make in India</span> <span>#Technology</span></div>
       <p>India's drone sector has experienced a remarkable surge in revenue, escalating from an estimated Rs 60 crore to a substantial Rs 3,000–4,000 crore. This impressive growth is attributed to the &quot;Make in India&quot; initiative, according to statements made by the Drone Federation Chief. The significant increase underscores the success of domestic manufacturing policies aimed at fostering technological advancement and self-reliance within the country.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-25/indias-drone-sector-sees-explosive-revenue-growth-under-make.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The &quot;Make in India&quot; programme has evidently provided a robust framework for the drone industry to flourish, enabling local manufacturers to scale production and innovate. This expansion is critical for various applications, including surveillance, logistics, agriculture, and potentially defence, showcasing the versatility and strategic importance of drone technology. The Drone Federation Chief's remarks highlight a sector that is not only growing rapidly but also contributing significantly to the national economy and technological landscape.</p><p>The sector's trajectory from a relatively nascent stage to a multi-crore industry in a short span reflects a concerted effort by both industry stakeholders and government support. The emphasis on indigenous development and production has created a vibrant ecosystem, attracting investment and fostering innovation. This growth positions India as a significant player in the global drone market, with potential for further expansion and diversification of applications.</p><p>What to watch: Further policy support for indigenous drone technology and new market applications.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article captures the details of the source summary accurately and maintains a professional tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener">Defence (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
