     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-09-25 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>iPhone 18 Pro Camera Faces Aperture Control Limitations</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tech</span> <span>#iPhone 18 Pro</span> <span>#Camera</span> <span>#Aperture</span></div>
       <p>The new main camera on the iPhone 18 Pro presents specific limitations regarding its variable aperture settings. Users are currently restricted to a choice of only four aperture values within the native iOS camera application's manual mode.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-25/iphone-18-pro-camera-faces-aperture-control-limitations.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>These available settings are f/1.48, f/1.8, f/2.8, and f/4. This constraint applies not only when users manually adjust the aperture but also when the camera app automatically controls the iris.</p><p>The findings highlight a notable aspect of the iPhone 18 Pro's camera functionality, indicating a fixed selection of aperture options rather than a broader, continuous range for photographers. This could influence how users approach depth of field and low-light photography directly through the default camera interface.</p><p>The restricted aperture choices are a key characteristic of the device's imaging system as it currently operates within the native software environment. This design decision impacts both user control and the automated adjustments made by the system.</p><p>What to watch: How third-party camera applications might address these native aperture limitations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately reflects the source material and maintains a professional tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1000729/moment-pro-blackmagic-camera-ii-ios-app-iphone-18-pro-max-aperature-camera" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
