       <h1>Microsoft's Copilot app adds Office, natural coding and automation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tech</span> <span>#Microsoft</span> <span>#Copilot</span> <span>#AI</span></div>
       <p>Microsoft's Copilot application is reportedly undergoing another significant update, described as a &quot;facelift&quot; for the platform. This refresh aims to enhance the app's capabilities and user experience.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-25/microsofts-copilot-app-adds-office-natural-coding-and-automa.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The latest iteration of Copilot is set to integrate new functionalities across several key areas. Among these additions are enhanced features related to Microsoft Office applications. This suggests a deeper synergy between Copilot and the suite of productivity tools, potentially streamlining workflows and user interactions within documents, spreadsheets, and presentations.</p><p>Furthermore, the update introduces capabilities for natural coding. This development points towards an improved ability for Copilot to assist developers and users in writing or understanding code through more intuitive, human-like language prompts. Such a feature could significantly lower the barrier to entry for coding tasks and accelerate development cycles.</p><p>Automation is another critical area receiving attention in this update. The inclusion of new automation features indicates that Copilot will be able to perform a wider range of tasks autonomously or with minimal user intervention. This could extend to routine operations, data processing, or orchestrating complex workflows.</p><p>These additions collectively represent a strategic evolution for the Copilot app. By expanding its utility into Office integration, natural coding, and automation, Microsoft appears to be positioning Copilot as a more versatile and integral assistant across various professional and personal computing tasks. The &quot;facelift&quot; signifies a concerted effort to broaden the application's appeal and practical utility.</p><p>What to watch: Observe how these new features enhance user productivity and developer workflows.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately elaborates on the source summary without introducing external claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2268096/microsofts-copilot-app-adds-office-natural-coding-and-automation/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
