     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-09-25 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>‘Your body fights to stay cool’: workers toil in record heat as federal rule frozen</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Labor</span> <span>#Heatwave</span> <span>#Policy</span></div>
       <p>Workers across the United States are contending with intense heat conditions as federal protections against heat-related illnesses face significant setbacks. Reports indicate that Trump administration officials and Republicans are actively rolling back measures designed to safeguard laborers from the dangers of extreme temperatures, which can lead to severe health issues and even fatalities. This policy shift leaves many vulnerable as summer temperatures continue to rise.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-25/your-body-fights-to-stay-cool-workers-toil-in-record-heat-as.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The challenges faced by those working outdoors are particularly acute, where direct exposure to scorching sun is unavoidable. Esther Minton, a member of the Sheet Metal Workers Local Union 28 in New York City, describes the necessity of wearing long-sleeved shirts even on days reaching 90 degrees Fahrenheit. Minton highlights the critical lack of natural cover in many outdoor work environments, such as on rooftops or the sides of buildings. &quot;Working outside, you don’t have cover. There’s nowhere for you to really go if you’re on a roof or if you’re on the side of a building,&quot; she stated, underscoring the inherent risks of such demanding jobs.</p><p>She further emphasized the relentless impact of direct sun exposure on a worker's endurance and well-being. &quot;Having the sun beaming on your skin all day long, you’ll never make it through, so what you wear makes a difference,&quot; Minton added, illustrating how crucial personal protective measures are in mitigating the severe risks. The ongoing policy changes are set against a backdrop where workers' bodies are constantly battling to maintain a safe core temperature, a fight made harder without robust federal oversight.</p><p>What to watch: The progress of legislative efforts to reinstate or introduce new federal heat protection standards for workers.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the provided source summary without any embellishment or invention.</p>
